    "created_at": "2025-04-28T18:31:49Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-04-28T18:31:49Z",
    "body": "> If it's useful to make the case, I worry the existing spec will diverge from many implementations which will want to implement this as a member boolean rather than a function pointer which returns a boolean, and so each implementation will have to introduce - by trial and error - the necessary attribute changed steps to sync the boolean.\n\nI'm agnostic (other than not wanting to be the one to put up a PR) to the approach. I'll point out that there are (I think?) many other places where the spec is written in a way that sounds easy to read/understand, but that diverges from actual implementations, and that's kind of \"ok\". Performance things come to mind - specs say \"iterate all descendants of a node\" because that's easy to understand, while implementations actually do lots of caching and stuff and don't actually iterate nodes. So maybe it seems ok to leave it?",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
